    "# Snare Sound\n",
    "The synthesis of the snare drum follows a procedure conceptually similar to that of the kick drum, beginning with the definition of a discrete-time vector, *t*. This vector represents the temporal axis over which the sound is constructed. Based on the length of *t*, a seperate array, referred to as *noise*, is generated, with each element assigned a pseudo-random value. Playback of this array alone would produce white noise, representing the characteristic \"snap\" component of the snare.\n",
    "\n",
    "To introduce a tonal element, a sine wave of the desired fundamental frequency - 180 hz in this case - is computed over the same time vector. Mathematically, the sine function takes the form $sin(2\\pi ft)$, where $f$ is the frequency and $t$ the time vector, analogous to the variable $x$ in $f(x)=sin(X)$. This produces a smooth oscillatory waveform that constitutes the tonal body of the snare.\n",
    "\n",
    "The dynamic evolution of the snare's amplitude is modeled using an exponential envelope with a relatively rapid decay, reflecting the natural damping of a percussive strike. Finally the snare sound is obtained by combining the tonal sine component and the white noise component in a weighted sum, modulated by the envelope. The envelope values range from 1 at the initial strike down to near 0 as the sound fades, ensuring a realistic response."
   ],
   "id": "3c0b5754ca3f1445"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {
