    "# Open Hi-Hat Sound\n",
    "The open hi-hat sound is a natural compliment to the closed hi-hat and thus make a great choice for a final fourth *instrument / sound* to be added to this down scale Roland TR-707 drum machine. The particular case of the 707 open hi-hat has special sounding almost 70's or 80's synthwave cowbell ish ring to it. It's characterristic timbre arises from a mixture of inharmonic partials and noise. In this project, the open hi-hat is synthesized by combining these elements with amplitude envelopes, filtering, and subtle nonlinear processing.\n",
    "\n",
    "The synthesis starts by generating metallic partials - sine waves at inharmonic frequencies. Unlike harmonic tones, inharmonic partials do not align with integer multiples of a fundemental, giving the sound it's bell-like, metallic quality. For example, partials at 500, 900, 1400, 2200, and 2800 hz create a rich inharmonic spectrum. To emulate the natural decay of a hi-hat, an exponential envelope is applied, with faster decay for higher frequencies. This ensures the sound remains crisp and short-lived, as expected for percussive strikes:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "freqs = [500, 900, 1400, 2200, 2800]\n",
    "metallic = sum(np.sin(2 * np.pi * f * t) for f in freqs)\n",
    "\n",
    "decay = np.exp(-t * 20)\n",
    "metallic *= decay\n",
    "```\n",
    "Next part of the hi-hat is pretty much just a closed hi-hat where some white noise with envelope is made as the tail to the first metallic part then as seen in the snare a weighted combined raw hi-hat sound is made. To emphasize the bright attack and remove unwanted low-frequency energy, the signal passes through the high-pass filter explained in the *High-Pass Filter* section. This highlights the transient peaks and prevents the hi-hat from sounding \"muddy\":\n",
    "```Python\n",
    "y = highpass(y, alpha=0.73)\n",
    "```\n",
    "as final step to really get that 707 open hi-hat sound to shine through soft clipping is applied using a hyperbolic tangent function. This introduces subtle harmonic distortion, adding grit and realism:\n",
    "```Python\n",
    "y = np.tanh(y * 3.0)\n",
    "```\n",
    "Then the result is normalized to ensure consistent amplitude."
   ],
