   "source": [
    "# The Sequencer\n",
    "the sequencer has five attributes:\n",
    " - \\+ beat : list[tuple[str,ANY] | ANY]\n",
    " - \\+ bpm : int\n",
    " - \\+ seconds_per_beat : float\n",
    " - \\+ Fs : int\n",
    "- - - - - - -\n",
    " - \\+ build_beat() : ndarray[tuple[int] , dtype[float64]]\n",
    "- - - - - - -\n",
    "## Running the program\n",
    "To run this program it first calls the tuple methods for making a [str, float] tuple array and then initializes the bpm at, in this case 140 bpm *beats per minute*, then it calculates seconds per beat $60/bpm$ and finally initializes\n",
    "```Fs ```\.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then the build_beat method is called with the beat array, sampling frequency and seconds per beat as inputs. This lets the function know the timing of each input from the beat array and also lets it know how many samples per second is being used. The method first calculates total beats by iterating the beat array and looking at the float part of the tuple on each tuple in the array and summarizing the numbers to get the total beats for the complete sequence. Then this number can be multiplied with seconds_per_beat and sampling frequency to get the total amount of samples needed to make the final output. Then an array is created of length total samples and filled with zeros; this is where all the sound values will be filled into. As the input in the beat array is intended to be duration in beats where 1 would be a 1/4 note duration, a relation between this number and the real-time duration has to be established like so *beat * seconds_per_beat*, then multiply this by the sampling frequency and typecast to int so we know how many samples will be needed for this. So then the sound method can be called with this input kick_sound(Fs, duration=beat * seconds_per_beat), and this is what happens for each entry in the beat array. The build_beat() method calls each sound synthesizing method and calculates how many samples the duration of the requested sound will last and fills the next amount of samples equal to the duration amount of samples with the output of the sound synthesizing method."

   ],
